erms I mean you say that there's  no majority for anything in the House of  Commons which is clearly true but the  reason there's no majority is because  MPs are just unwilling to compromise  from their positions we can have a  little bit of a listen to what one of  your former colleagues Nick Bowles said  after he decided on Monday to quit the  Conservative Party chiefly because my  party refuses to compromise  I regret therefore to announce that I  can no longer sit for this part I mean  he's right isn't he and you didn't  compromise either did you you didn't  vote for anything no I actually know I  actually abstained on the options  because that if Parliament wanted to  agree on his options fine I think one it  is a long-standing friend of mine but  what we've got to realise is every  different brexit path represents a very  different future for our country those  who want a new deal brexit see the  economic future of our country  ultimately as a trade deal with Donald  Trump those who feel they want to  protect manufacturing jobs in the north  won't a customs union with the EU so if  these are irreconcilable choices about  the future in which were direction in  which we take the country you can't  split the difference you can't split the  difference if you feel that to protect  manufacturing jobs in the Midlands the  most important thing is frictionless  trade so what will you compromise on  them because it feels as if the people  might want to second my friend Irma just  as intransigent is done in France I  think second referendum is not a brexit  outcome it's a process  what would none of us knew at the time  of the refers referendum was what was  negotiable my party set its red lines  not even knowing what she want then  other than a second referendum I mean  most people would say that that is some  people would view that as just a way of  saying that actually you want to remain  but you just don't want to say it and I  would campaign for remain in a second  